It comes from the device's MAC address, not from a photo. The first three pairs of a MAC are a block the IEEE assigns to one company, so RackTrack can name who built a device it has never seen — that is how a MAC hanging off a port gets labelled Dell, Realtek, Apple and so on. It is the maker of the network chip, not necessarily the model of the box.

"Unknown" means one of:
- the block is registered privately, so the owner paid to keep the name hidden and there is nothing to show;
- the block is newer than RackTrack's copy of the registry;
- the address is locally administered or randomised (common on phones and some virtual machines), so it belongs to nobody.

There is nothing to fix on your side — it does not affect port state, cabling or anything else you can act on. Treat the vendor name as a hint for identifying what is plugged in, and confirm from the device itself before acting on it.

